1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:03,220
You step onto a Jakarta commuter
train that started its working life in

2
00:00:03,260 --> 00:00:04,700
Tokyo decades ago.

3
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:08,039
In November, Jakarta
refused to buy any more.

4
00:00:09,133 --> 00:00:13,033
The reason it came here is simple, and
it works like hand-me-down clothes.

5
00:00:13,813 --> 00:00:17,253
They only fit if the size matches,
and they only help while there's life

6
00:00:17,313 --> 00:00:17,813
left in them.

7
00:00:18,513 --> 00:00:20,593
The reason it stopped is a rule about age.

8
00:00:21,767 --> 00:00:23,896
Four players run this story.

9
00:00:23,896 --> 00:00:27,640
KAI, the state railway
behind the commuter trains.

10
00:00:27,640 --> 00:00:30,944
The government, which
sets the import rules.

11
00:00:30,944 --> 00:00:33,660
INKA, Indonesia's own train builder.

12
00:00:33,660 --> 00:00:36,669
And CRRC, a Chinese maker of new trains.

13
00:00:36,669 --> 00:00:39,899
We'll cover why Tokyo's
trains fit Jakarta,

14
00:00:39,899 --> 00:00:45,186
why the deal worked for twenty-five
years, and why it ended in November

15
00:00:45,186 --> 00:00:45,626
2026.

16
00:00:46,780 --> 00:00:48,419
It started with a gift.

17
00:00:48,419 --> 00:00:53,198
In May 2000, Japan's aid agency and
the Tokyo city government donated

18
00:00:53,198 --> 00:00:56,816
seventy-two used subway
cars from Tokyo's Mita Line.

19
00:00:57,967 --> 00:00:59,986
Those cars were built from 1968.

20
00:00:59,986 --> 00:01:03,413
Tokyo retired them after
about thirty years of service,

21
00:01:03,413 --> 00:01:05,984
and Jakarta kept them running until 2016.

22
00:01:07,120 --> 00:01:10,120
They were the first air-conditioned
electric trains in Indonesia.

23
00:01:11,019 --> 00:01:14,780
At the time, crowded riders hung out
of open doors or sat on the roof.

24
00:01:15,900 --> 00:01:18,026
Then Jakarta started buying them.

25
00:01:18,026 --> 00:01:22,779
In 2014, the operator said a used car
from Japan cost eight hundred million

26
00:01:22,779 --> 00:01:24,217
to one billion rupiah.

27
00:01:25,280 --> 00:01:27,463
A new car cost ten to twelve billion.

28
00:01:27,463 --> 00:01:30,560
Same seats, same doors,
about one tenth of the price.

29
00:01:31,680 --> 00:01:36,016
Indonesia's own train maker, INKA,
quoted about one point three million

30
00:01:36,016 --> 00:01:37,883
dollars for a new car in 2019.

31
00:01:37,883 --> 00:01:41,917
Even after shipping, a used car
was about one ninth of that price.

32
00:01:43,067 --> 00:01:45,364
For Japan, the deal was just as good.

33
00:01:45,364 --> 00:01:47,238
Scrapping a train costs money.

34
00:01:47,238 --> 00:01:51,647
Shipping it to Jakarta cost less, and
somebody else would keep using it.

35
00:01:53,500 --> 00:01:55,080
But price alone doesn't explain it.

36
00:01:55,700 --> 00:01:58,940
In many countries, a train from Tokyo
can't run without major changes.

37
00:01:59,580 --> 00:02:00,880
In Jakarta, it can.

38
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:04,641
The first size that matches is the rails.

39
00:02:04,641 --> 00:02:09,484
Japan's regular lines and Java's lines
are both one thousand and sixty-seven

40
00:02:09,484 --> 00:02:10,679
millimetres apart.

41
00:02:10,679 --> 00:02:13,635
Change the rail width
and you need new wheels,

42
00:02:13,635 --> 00:02:15,520
new axles, often a new train.

43
00:02:16,687 --> 00:02:17,927
That match has a history.

44
00:02:19,267 --> 00:02:22,887
Java's first railway in
1867 was built wider.

45
00:02:24,047 --> 00:02:26,707
During the Japanese
occupation in the 1940s,

46
00:02:27,207 --> 00:02:30,987
the remaining wide lines on Java were
converted to the narrower Japanese

47
00:02:31,087 --> 00:02:31,387
width.

48
00:02:32,387 --> 00:02:35,607
That is a big reason a Tokyo
train fits Java's rails today.

49
00:02:36,700 --> 00:02:38,244
The power matches too.

50
00:02:38,244 --> 00:02:42,808
Jakarta's electric line opened in
1925 on fifteen hundred volts DC,

51
00:02:42,808 --> 00:02:46,498
the same system the 205
series was built for in Tokyo.

52
00:02:47,633 --> 00:02:49,373
Same rails, same power.

53
00:02:50,313 --> 00:02:53,553
A Tokyo train arrives by ship,
gets new signs and checks,

54
00:02:53,973 --> 00:02:56,413
and goes into service with
no rebuilding of the tracks.

55
00:02:57,467 --> 00:02:58,853
So Jakarta kept buying.

56
00:02:58,853 --> 00:03:03,125
In 2012, the operator planned to bring
in one hundred and forty cars that

57
00:03:03,125 --> 00:03:06,646
year, and aimed for fourteen
hundred and forty cars by 2019.

58
00:03:07,767 --> 00:03:09,087
The biggest batch came later.

59
00:03:10,007 --> 00:03:15,127
JR East's 205 series, a Tokyo
commuter train first built in 1985,

60
00:03:15,527 --> 00:03:18,007
started running in Jakarta in 2013.

61
00:03:19,133 --> 00:03:21,312
Today the backbone is still Japanese.

62
00:03:21,312 --> 00:03:24,867
Seven hundred and eighty cars
came from JR East's 205 series,

63
00:03:24,867 --> 00:03:27,791
and one hundred and
twenty-eight from Tokyo Metro.

64
00:03:28,933 --> 00:03:31,772
Those cars are thirty-four
to forty-one years old.

65
00:03:31,772 --> 00:03:35,333
Many of them started work in
Tokyo before the Berlin Wall fell.

66
00:03:37,213 --> 00:03:40,933
A fleet that old needs replacing,
and passenger numbers kept rising.

67
00:03:41,693 --> 00:03:45,473
The first attempt to replace it
with more Tokyo trains came in 2023,

68
00:03:45,853 --> 00:03:46,573
and it was blocked.

69
00:03:47,667 --> 00:03:52,781
The operator asked to import three
hundred and forty-eight used cars of a

70
00:03:52,781 --> 00:03:56,306
newer Japanese model, the
E217, for 2023 and 2024.

71
00:03:57,367 --> 00:03:58,467
The government said no.

72
00:03:58,967 --> 00:04:00,367
The cars stayed in Japan.

73
00:04:01,027 --> 00:04:04,127
Officials chose to refit old
trains and buy new ones instead.

74
00:04:05,213 --> 00:04:08,653
New trains came from two
places, a Chinese maker,

75
00:04:09,113 --> 00:04:12,513
CRRC Sifang, and INKA in East Java.

76
00:04:13,633 --> 00:04:16,592
INKA also took a contract to
refit nineteen of the old trains,

77
00:04:16,592 --> 00:04:19,313
so they could keep running
while the new ones were built.

78
00:04:20,480 --> 00:04:24,211
In January 2024, the operator
signed for three Chinese trains.

79
00:04:24,211 --> 00:04:28,002
The contract was seven hundred
and eighty-three billion rupiah,

80
00:04:28,002 --> 00:04:31,020
about two hundred and
sixty-one billion per train.

81
00:04:32,133 --> 00:04:33,422
The new trains are longer.

82
00:04:33,422 --> 00:04:36,903
Each one has twelve cars and can carry
about three thousand four hundred

83
00:04:36,903 --> 00:04:37,713
people per trip.

84
00:04:38,847 --> 00:04:40,682
Compare that with the old deal.

85
00:04:40,682 --> 00:04:44,123
Twelve used cars from Japan
once cost about twelve billion.

86
00:04:44,123 --> 00:04:47,505
One new twelve-car train cost
around twenty times as much.

87
00:04:48,647 --> 00:04:50,931
New trains fix one problem: age.

88
00:04:50,931 --> 00:04:55,291
In November 2025, three of the
oldest Japanese series retired,

89
00:04:55,291 --> 00:04:57,987
some of them close to fifty years old.

90
00:04:59,100 --> 00:05:02,975
Their driver cabins are going on
display at Jakarta Kota station.

91
00:05:02,975 --> 00:05:06,440
Jakarta gave its second-hand
trains a retirement ceremony.

92
00:05:08,333 --> 00:05:11,013
So the plan was new trains
from China and from INKA.

93
00:05:11,953 --> 00:05:13,533
The weak point was INKA's factory.

94
00:05:14,647 --> 00:05:18,626
All eleven Chinese trains are running,
mostly on the Bogor and Cikarang

95
00:05:18,626 --> 00:05:19,013
lines.

96
00:05:19,013 --> 00:05:22,938
Here, a Chinese train on the Bogor
line shares the tracks with the old

97
00:05:22,938 --> 00:05:23,767
Japanese ones.

98
00:05:24,867 --> 00:05:27,377
INKA was expected to
build eight trains a year.

99
00:05:27,377 --> 00:05:28,786
It delivered two to three.

100
00:05:29,900 --> 00:05:34,013
By September 2026, seven of the
sixteen INKA trains were in service.

101
00:05:34,013 --> 00:05:37,051
That left nine trains the
network was counting on,

102
00:05:37,051 --> 00:05:38,660
still not carrying anyone.

103
00:05:39,747 --> 00:05:41,870
Meanwhile the trains got more crowded.

104
00:05:41,870 --> 00:05:45,897
Ridership in the first half of 2026
was up about eleven percent on a year

105
00:05:45,897 --> 00:05:46,387
earlier.

106
00:05:47,520 --> 00:05:49,973
That's about one point
one million trips a day.

107
00:05:49,973 --> 00:05:52,987
The operator had planned for
three to four percent growth,

108
00:05:52,987 --> 00:05:53,600
not eleven.

109
00:05:54,713 --> 00:05:57,813
On the busiest days, the count
can reach one point three million.

110
00:05:57,813 --> 00:06:00,673
That's two hundred thousand
more trips than an ordinary day.

111
00:06:01,767 --> 00:06:04,772
The operator calculated it needs
one hundred and five trains.

112
00:06:04,772 --> 00:06:06,127
It has one hundred and two.

113
00:06:07,247 --> 00:06:09,138
And the gap was about to grow.

114
00:06:09,138 --> 00:06:13,103
Thirteen former Tokyo Metro trains
were due to retire this year.

115
00:06:14,213 --> 00:06:16,933
A new train takes one to two
years from order to delivery.

116
00:06:17,813 --> 00:06:22,293
An order signed in January 2026
would arrive in 2027 at the earliest.

117
00:06:24,133 --> 00:06:27,573
So in September 2026,
Jakarta went back to Tokyo.

118
00:06:28,693 --> 00:06:33,835
On September 23, the head of the national
railway asked parliament to allow

119
00:06:33,835 --> 00:06:37,893
used Japanese trains again, as
a stopgap for 2026 and 2027.

120
00:06:39,033 --> 00:06:40,815
Parliament's committee agreed.

121
00:06:40,815 --> 00:06:43,401
The order would be
twenty-three used trains,

122
00:06:43,401 --> 00:06:47,251
on one condition: keep building
up Indonesia's own train industry.

123
00:06:48,367 --> 00:06:53,090
The pressure behind that vote was a
forecast of three hundred and sixty-nine

124
00:06:53,090 --> 00:06:55,114
million passenger trips in 2026.

125
00:06:55,114 --> 00:06:58,365
Every missing train makes
the remaining ones fuller.

126
00:06:58,365 --> 00:07:01,493
Everyone in this story
wanted something different.

127
00:07:01,493 --> 00:07:04,007
The railway wanted trains within months.

128
00:07:04,007 --> 00:07:07,442
Parliament wanted the home
train industry kept growing.

129
00:07:07,442 --> 00:07:09,832
Japan wanted old trains off its books.

130
00:07:09,832 --> 00:07:13,940
And the trade ministry had a cap
on how old a used machine can be.

131
00:07:15,067 --> 00:07:16,727
To be fair, the argument was time.

132
00:07:17,327 --> 00:07:19,487
A used train could arrive
in one to two months.

133
00:07:20,207 --> 00:07:21,807
A new one takes one to two years.

134
00:07:22,953 --> 00:07:24,129
Then it hit a rule.

135
00:07:24,129 --> 00:07:28,537
Indonesia's trade ministry only allows
used capital goods like these up to

136
00:07:28,537 --> 00:07:29,593
twenty years old.

137
00:07:30,667 --> 00:07:34,106
The trains on offer were twenty-five
to twenty-eight years old.

138
00:07:34,106 --> 00:07:37,706
On average, they had been carrying
passengers in Japan since 1998.

139
00:07:38,833 --> 00:07:41,593
The transport ministry
added another problem.

140
00:07:41,593 --> 00:07:43,033
Hand-me-downs wear out.

141
00:07:43,033 --> 00:07:47,533
A train used for twenty to thirty years
in Japan might have only about ten

142
00:07:47,533 --> 00:07:48,253
years left.

143
00:07:48,253 --> 00:07:51,613
After that, Jakarta would be
shopping for trains again.

144
00:07:52,747 --> 00:07:54,767
Old trains are also
harder to keep running.

145
00:07:56,087 --> 00:07:58,787
When three Japanese
series retired in 2025,

146
00:07:59,387 --> 00:08:02,087
one stated reason was that
their spare parts had run out.

147
00:08:03,180 --> 00:08:07,608
On November 5, the railway's chief
executive cancelled the plan.

148
00:08:07,608 --> 00:08:10,539
His words: for the public, it must be new.

149
00:08:12,387 --> 00:08:14,087
You might guess Japan ran out of trains.

150
00:08:14,907 --> 00:08:15,327
It didn't.

151
00:08:16,147 --> 00:08:19,787
Japan still has trains to sell, but
they are too old for Indonesia's own

152
00:08:19,847 --> 00:08:20,147
rule.

153
00:08:21,167 --> 00:08:24,389
Japan keeps its commuter trains
for twenty to thirty years.

154
00:08:24,389 --> 00:08:28,146
By the time a car is free to sell,
it's usually past Jakarta's limit.

155
00:08:29,267 --> 00:08:31,987
The window that opened in
2000 closed on its own.

156
00:08:32,827 --> 00:08:36,147
The supply that made Jakarta's network
cheap now ages out before it can be

157
00:08:36,207 --> 00:08:36,507
shipped.

158
00:08:37,600 --> 00:08:39,160
So what do commuters ride next?

159
00:08:40,040 --> 00:08:43,180
The operator had three
options: order from INKA,

160
00:08:43,640 --> 00:08:46,500
keep the old trains running
longer, or import used ones.

161
00:08:47,653 --> 00:08:50,443
With imports off the
table, that leaves two.

162
00:08:50,443 --> 00:08:54,907
Old Japanese cars stay in service
longer, and INKA has to build faster.

163
00:08:54,907 --> 00:08:57,573
Some of those cars are already past forty.

164
00:08:58,733 --> 00:09:01,479
The money for new trains
was already approved.

165
00:09:01,479 --> 00:09:05,979
A year earlier, the government set aside
five trillion rupiah for thirty new

166
00:09:05,979 --> 00:09:07,553
trains, due within a year.

167
00:09:09,400 --> 00:09:13,166
The longer plan is to replace
eighty-four old Japanese trains with

168
00:09:13,166 --> 00:09:17,269
INKA-built ones, and raise capacity
from one point one to one point four

169
00:09:17,269 --> 00:09:19,180
million passengers a day by 2030.

170
00:09:21,033 --> 00:09:25,390
For twenty-five years, Tokyo's old
trains let Jakarta grow a rail network it

171
00:09:25,390 --> 00:09:26,974
couldn't afford to buy new.

172
00:09:26,974 --> 00:09:29,521
Then the hand-me-downs
aged out of the rule,

173
00:09:29,521 --> 00:09:32,125
and the next trains have
to be built at home.

174
00:09:32,125 --> 00:09:35,125
So the refusal in November
came from Tokyo's closet,

175
00:09:35,125 --> 00:09:37,729
which had nothing left
that was young enough.

176
00:09:39,600 --> 00:09:43,280
Those trains carry people to and from
one island that holds more than half

177
00:09:43,320 --> 00:09:43,980
of Indonesia.

178
00:09:44,840 --> 00:09:48,240
Next video, why so many
Indonesians live on Java.
